# 1.6 Git Branching & Collaboration

## 1. Create a repository and make the initial commit

I created a Git repository for the **FoodieReview** app on the `main` branch. Its `README.md` describes the project as an app for sharing food reviews and ratings.

```bash
git init -b main
git add README.md
git commit -m "Initial commit for FoodieReview"
```

## 2. Create a feature branch and add a rating function

I created the `feature/add-rating` branch and added `rating.js`:

```javascript
function addRating() {
  console.log("Sample rating: 5 stars");
}

module.exports = { addRating };
```

I committed the change:

```bash
git add rating.js
git commit -m "Add sample rating function"
```

## 3. Create and resolve a merge conflict

On `main`, I added the comment `// main branch update` to the function line and committed it. On `feature/add-rating`, I added `// feature branch update` to that same line and committed it. Merging the feature branch into `main` caused a conflict in `rating.js`.

I resolved the conflict by keeping both comments:

```javascript
function addRating() { // main branch update
  // feature branch update
  console.log("Sample rating: 5 stars");
}

module.exports = { addRating };
```

Then I committed the resolution:

```bash
git add rating.js
git commit -m "Resolve rating.js merge conflict"
```

## 4. Push the branch and open a Pull Request

After creating a GitHub repository named `FoodieReview` and authenticating, push the branches:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/FoodieReview.git
git push -u origin main
git push -u origin feature/add-rating
```

Create a Pull Request from `feature/add-rating` to `main` with this description:

> Added the `addRating()` sample function. I resolved the merge conflict in `rating.js` by preserving both the main branch and feature branch comments.

## 5. Fork an open-source repository and propose an Indian-currency adaptation

Fork a public calculator repository on GitHub, then clone your fork and create a branch:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git
cd REPOSITORY
git switch -c feature/indian-currency
```

Add a comment to a relevant file describing the proposed adaptation, for example:

```javascript
// For Indian users, format currency in INR (₹) and support lakh and crore groupings.
```

Commit and push the branch:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Document Indian currency adaptation"
git push -u origin feature/indian-currency
```

Then open a Pull Request from your fork’s `feature/indian-currency` branch to the original repository’s default branch.

**Note:** The FoodieReview repository and merge resolution were completed locally. Publishing the branches and completing the fork and Pull Request steps require GitHub authentication.